{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the DX-A2 repair at head 7424aafc8ef80ec0287d6941bd42e388a98482a9, tree 23a884d4, for request 1c124ebf and review request dc1154e5. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, for two remaining items.\n\nThe head adds five commits to the reviewed head aa20f0fe. I read the note first, then the diff, against the six items of review 0924ce2e and ruling 366b3989. An independent read of the repaired source ran beside mine, and I confirmed both items below in the code.\n\nThe repair resolves nearly everything. Five of the six items hold, and so does most of the sixth. Two gaps remain against the ruling.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. A causal signed reader below the floor gets `forbidden`, not `archived`.\n   - `Reads.minimum` refuses when the row is gone and the limit is an unscoped key (`reads.ts:211-214`). `Reads.entry` answers `archived` before any limit check (`reads.ts:332-333`), so the barrier is stricter than the read it guards.\n   - Scenario: a key signs entry N, the intent window passes, and a floor passes N. `followReceipt` sends `entry(N, {at, head: N})`, the barrier returns `forbidden`, and the handle doesn't fall back, because it falls back only on `unavailable`. Main answers `archived`, and a plain `entry(N)` at this head still does.\n   - Ruling 366b3989: \"entry answers archived with the floor, commit and through position, as main does today\". The floor case in `application-projection-native.test.ts` uses only a session reader.\n   - Fix: answer `archived` for that position. Don't simply admit archived rows into the wait, because a stranger could then call `entry(archived, {head: 99})` and read `behind.current`. Admit them only when the minimum's position is also below the floor, or answer without waiting. Add a native case with a causal reader below a real floor, and a case for the stranger.\n\n2. The hosted enrollment 409 no longer covers a hash mismatch at or above the floor.\n   - `packages/mcp/src/hosted.ts:550` still sends a full-head minimum. A wrong retained hash therefore returns `unavailable` from the barrier, then 503 `unresolved` at line 551. Main returned 409 from line 553. The 409 now covers only an input mismatch and a kept row below the floor. This repair doesn't change the file.\n   - Ruling 366b3989 says that the 409 is preserved, and the note says it's retained. No test covers `rule: \"entry\"`.\n   - Fix: send a qualified sequence minimum here, as `followReceipt` does, and add a case for each 409 path. If the planner accepts 503 for this case, correct the note.\n\nBoth items change production source, so one further full gate must run at the final head. Every earlier gate failure stays failed, and repair gate 4 stays a pass for its source.\n\n## Items of 0924ce2e\n\n1. Below the floor. Resolved apart from item 1 above.\n   - A full minimum strictly below the floor orders only. The floor is read in the same synchronous step as the hash, so a floor that advances during a wait is safe. A position exactly at the floor needs the exact hash, and the native case covers that boundary. A wrong retained hash at or above the floor stays `unavailable`.\n   - The card refresh carries its old receipt past a real floor and still answers. Below the floor a right and a wrong hash get the same answer, so there is no oracle.\n2. The flight. Resolved. The wait now precedes `PreparationFlights`, and a native case shows an item query succeeding during a controlled wait. The added `earlierEnds` argument only shortens the deadline, and other callers pass nothing.\n3. Causal readers. Resolved for readers who get to wait. The chains refresh on admission and after each wake, a seal during a refresh causes another, and authority still runs before any head.\n4. Refusals. Resolved for `followReceipt`, and the second item above is the hosted route. It sends a qualified sequence minimum and checks the returned reference and the computed hash afterwards, so `hash-mismatch` and `reference-mismatch` are back over real transports. `client.test.ts` restores the original assertions. The single `unavailable` fallback is one ordinary read, with no loop.\n5. Transports. Resolved. The CLI wrappers carry all five minima, and the dev host refuses a valid one with `unavailable`. The handle rejects a reply below its minimum and an equal head with another hash. No legitimate reply is rejected, because `at` is the current head on all five reads, including cursor pages.\n6. Kernel text. Resolved. Section 7 names `read-minimum.ts` and its five exports, `isBehindRead`, the `ScopeApi` argument, `BehindRead`, the bound and both projection refusals, with the below-floor rule in one sentence.\n\nThe note records the single-file exception as rejected and the waiter cap as deferred, as the ruling says.\n\n## The gates\n\n- Repair gate 4 at 713487e0, tree f2dc10bd, passes all five stages. Its receipt exits 0 with complete capture, the same head and tree before and after, and no dropped bytes. Plain passes 3,156 with one skip and Native 866 with two skips.\n- From 713487e0 to the head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- The test-only commits are true repairs. The Counter fake replied below the entry it returned, which a real service can't do. `legacy-lending-projection` is a stand-in, with no production path issuing those queries concurrently. `lending.test.ts` only adds labels and diagnostics, with no assertion removed. The note says that the original Lending failure's cause stays unconfirmed.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 5068dab4 gives bcbe0160, with no conflict.\n- I ran the owning files once each, with no whole suite: client and bytes `read-minimum` and `intent`, 19 of 19; native `read-minimum`, `client`, `application-projection-native` and `preparation-flight`, 13 of 13; `security-entry-points.test.mjs`, 10 of 10. The builder worktree stayed clean.\n- `node scripts/check-kernel-boundary.mjs` checks 127 production sources with no refusals.\n- The evidence verifies. The directory has 1,378 files and 289,443,970 bytes, its `SHA256SUMS` has digest 2ee295f3 and reruns clean, and no file is unlisted or writable. The external full manifest matches its digest, and all 1,378 of its rows match. The committed manifest, at 40,389 bytes, is under the limit.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 761 links, 0 broken. check-notes: 0 warnings. check-commit-messages: 10 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. A seal arriving during the final check after the timer fired returns `unavailable`, even if the head reached the minimum or `behind` was due (`read-wait.ts:40-41`). This needs an asynchronous chain update, so it mainly affects signed readers in busy rooms.\n2. An unscoped causal reader gets no wait for a future entry. This protects the head from strangers and matches the plain read, but the \"sealed during the wait\" guarantee covers only readers who get to wait. The note could say so.\n3. The two `#prepared` calls in `#projectionMinimum` now run outside the flight's timer, so the server can reach about 20 seconds before `active()` refuses. The client's 10-second limit still applies.\n4. A throw from `reads.minimum` in a projection now rejects, where the flight turned it into `unavailable`. The likely HTTP result is 500.\n5. Each wake runs `#rooted` per waiter, and waiters don't share chain updates. The one-second bound limits this, and the planner has deferred the waiter cap.\n6. No test covers a floor advancing during a wait, or a kept row with a wrong receipt hash.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe wait sits outside the flight, the barrier stays in Scope, and the handle owns receipt identity. That is the right split. Approved, subject to item 1.\n\n## Security\n\nAuthority holds at every wake, and the below-floor path grants nothing and reveals no hash. Items 1 and 2 change what honest readers are told, not what they may read: a reader loses the archive pointer, and a permanent mismatch reads as retryable. Changes requested, items 1 and 2.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe sequence minimum in `followReceipt` removes the conflict between the barrier and the handle's own checks. The same change simplifies the hosted route. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved, subject to item 1.\n- Security: changes requested, items 1 and 2.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: the repairs hold apart from the two items above, and the evidence verifies. Items 1 and 2 each need a fix and a case, and one further full gate.\n\nA head that makes these changes can come back for a review of its diff from this head. Disposition of 0924ce2e: resolved, apart from these two points.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f8b5e1ead9a558e372f9f6fa227c1b9a98a8c0b8","binding":"assigned","head":"7424aafc8ef80ec0287d6941bd42e388a98482a9","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bd11e303291bc9cb9afec5feff3e19361be29d2a\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:28df5171acd1320942be751570e7a2b044a541e1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:669945873d18e2acef6cb120aa2849ed8e1b7b4b","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}